GIVEN A MACHINE: ITS OBJECTS, PROCESSES, AND EXECUTION.

Document status: English Major C experimental normative-draft specification.
Scope: symbolic ARMv7-A application-level execution in T32 (Thumb) state.
Cross-reference: "All and only these initial operations are permitted.english"
in this directory. Precise ARM source and gaps are recorded in
"Instruction set architecture - sources and unfulfilled coverage.english".

PART ONE. THE DECLARATION OF THE MACHINE.

Given a machine satisfying the ARMv7-A architecture and capable of executing
16-bit and 32-bit Thumb instructions (T32), operating for the present
description in its unprivileged application mode, the following properties
shall be assumed and no additional physical property shall be inferred.

The machine has an execution index.

Execution index: A value identifying the memory address from which the next
instruction is to be fetched. Ordinary sequential execution advances this
value according to the length of the instruction. Branching replaces it with
the address of another instruction.

The expression "execution index" is the preferred English Major C name for
the abstract next-instruction address. Its value is a byte address, not an
ordinal count of instructions and not the number of instructions completed.
This agreed wording is also tracked, unmodified, in PR #2, "Define the
execution index". The execution index is not a new hardware register.

The machine has thirteen architecturally visible general-purpose registers
called R0 through R12, each holding exactly 32 bits. R13 has the special role
of stack pointer, abbreviated SP. R14 has the special role of link register,
abbreviated LR. R15 has the special role of program counter, abbreviated PC.
Their physical implementations, renaming, banked copies, and issue pipelines
are not specified by this declaration. R0-R12 hold patterns of 32 bits; they
do not carry an intrinsic "signed", "pointer", "text", or "instruction" tag.

The machine has architectural condition state, including N (negative), Z
(zero), C (carry or no-borrow according to the producing arithmetic operation),
and V (signed overflow), as described in the application program status
register, APSR. Other status fields, the Q bit, GE bits, and system status
registers exist in the architecture but are NOT licensed for direct
English Major C use by the initial operation set merely by naming APSR here.

The machine has access to a byte-addressed virtual memory view. A byte has
eight bits. Reading or writing multiple bytes depends on the mapping,
permissions, alignment rules, size of access, endianness, and architectural
memory behavior applicable to the exact operation. A load from a virtual
address is NOT a promise of a physical DRAM transfer: it may be served by
a cache, may generate a translation/access exception, or may interact with
memory of a different type. The processor's memory controller, interconnect,
cache hierarchy, page tables, and DRAM are distinct objects. An application
does not by default know their physical addresses or part numbers.

The machine has a mechanism for fetching instruction halfwords, decoding
the instruction in the current T32 execution state, checking architectural
conditions and relevant exceptions, executing its effects, and determining
the architectural next instruction. This declaration does not assert any
particular number of physical pipeline stages, prefetch distance, execution
units, branch-prediction policy, or latency.

The machine has an instruction set architecture: the full architectural
reference is ARM DDI 0406C.d, whose identity is provided in the other
referenced file. The separate closed English Major C operation grant names
which instructions a program in this initial language profile MAY request.
The actual processor may support many instructions that the language profile
does not yet permit. These two sets MUST NOT be conflated.

PART TWO. THE ABSTRACT PROGRAM STATE.

For purposes of explaining a single architectural instruction, define the
state as the tuple of:
  (a) the execution index, in byte-address units;
  (b) the 32-bit values of R0-R12, SP, and LR;
  (c) the architecturally relevant N, Z, C, and V flags;
  (d) current T32 conditional-execution state, including ITSTATE when active;
  (e) the architecturally observable memory contents and attributes;
  (f) instruction and data translation, permissions, and execution state;
  (g) any exception state necessary to represent a fault or trap.

This tuple is a specification model, not a claim that the full physical
microarchitecture is expressible by that tuple. A later concurrency contract
must add architecturally relevant sharing, atomicity, ordering, exclusive
monitor state, and observer relations. In their absence, this file claims
only a single-threaded trace under the stated memory-access assumptions.

An ordinary successful instruction transition may modify only those
components that its exact operation allows. An instruction that completes
normally advances the execution index according to the T32 encoding length,
unless the operation explicitly transfers control. An instruction that
generates an exception follows its architectural exception semantics;
it is not equivalent to an ordinary completed transition.

PART THREE. INSTRUCTION LENGTH AND THE REGISTER CALLED PC.

In the selected T32 state a single instruction occupies one or two 16-bit
halfwords: two bytes or four bytes. The sequential execution index normally
advances by TWO BYTES or FOUR BYTES, never by "one instruction" as though
every instruction had the same byte length.

The architectural value obtained when a T32 instruction reads PC as an
operand is ordinarily the address of that instruction plus four bytes,
subject to the specified operation's alignment and special rules. Certain
PC-relative operations align that value to a four-byte boundary. This
operand-reading behavior MUST NOT be mistaken for the value of the
abstract execution index, or for the address of whatever halfword a
physical prefetch unit may already have fetched. A program-counter write
is a control transfer and is governed by its specific instruction rules.

Instruction fetch alignment and branch-target restrictions are those of
ARM DDI 0406C.d. In particular, a T32 instruction address denotes a
halfword-aligned instruction boundary. A four-byte instruction may start at
a halfword address that is not divisible by four. A branch must not be
declared valid solely because its destination address contains bytes:
the destination must be a permitted instruction boundary with appropriate
execution state and access rights.

PART FOUR. WHEN BYTES ARE DATA AND WHEN THEY ARE INSTRUCTIONS.

The same memory bytes may be interpreted as integer data, encoded text,
an address, or instruction encodings. None of those meanings is an
intrinsic property of the byte sequence. Their meaning is established by
the operation performing the read and by the applicable representation
and execution contract.

A data-load operation obtains data from memory and does not itself request
that the loaded bytes be executed. A legal instruction-fetch operation
obtains bytes from an executable mapping in the machine's instruction
stream and decodes them according to the current execution state.
A branch changes the next architectural instruction address; it does
not magically make a non-executable mapping executable. The availability
of writable or executable page permissions belongs to the operating
environment. An Android user process cannot assume arbitrary changes
to page permissions, code-cache coherency, or authority to execute data.

Self-modifying or generated machine code, instructions stored in ordinary
buffers, and transitions through newly generated code are therefore
NOT implicitly permitted by this first profile. Any later provision must
specify memory permissions, data-cache and instruction-cache maintenance,
required synchronization, instruction boundary validation, and OS effects
rather than treating a cast from "data" to "code" as sufficient.

PART FIVE. SEQUENTIAL EXECUTION, BRANCHING, AND CONDITIONAL EXECUTION.

For a successfully executed non-branch instruction of length L bytes,
the default architectural successor is the address indexed before
execution plus L, where L is either two or four. The program must not
assume a single uniform instruction width.

A branch operation instead selects an architectural target under a
specified condition. A conditional branch whose condition is false
continues sequentially. The evaluated condition is derived from the
architectural condition flags, or from the source register for CBZ/CBNZ,
according to the precise instruction. Equality after subtraction, for
example, is determined from the Z flag.

The IT instruction establishes a bounded conditional-execution block for
following T32 instructions, using an initial condition and permitted inverse
conditions. It is NOT an ordinary unrestricted if statement: its length,
legal instruction placement, flag behavior, and branch placement are
constrained by the ARM architecture. The initial operation grant cannot
legalize an otherwise invalid or unpredictable IT sequence.

The execution index describes architectural control flow. Physical
instruction prefetch, cache fills, branch speculation, branch prediction,
and any discarded speculative work do NOT change the language's declared
result unless the architecture explicitly exposes an observable effect.
A one-time source-level dispatch between two loops is a distinct
program transformation; it must not be called "hardware branch prefetch".

PART SIX. LOADS, STORES, FLAGS, AND EXCEPTIONS.

A register-to-register arithmetic operation works with architectural
register values rather than directly adding values stored in DRAM.
A load obtains a memory value into registers, possibly using caches and
translation hardware; a store requests the reverse effect. The operation
specification determines address formation, size, sign extension or zero
extension, effective endianness, writeback, alignment, and fault behavior.
If any material property remains unspecified, no executable lowering
may be pronounced verified.

Arithmetic that writes N, Z, C, or V is different from arithmetic that
preserves those flags. In particular, a compare is not an invisible
mathematical predicate: it has prescribed flag effects on later
conditional branches. Any substitution involving conditional execution
must preserve all architecturally observable relevant effects.

An exception, including an invalid instruction, instruction fetch fault,
permission fault, data fault, or supervisor call, is NOT represented by
silently skipping the instruction. The exact handling differs between
an operating-system-controlled user process and privileged firmware.
This file specifies no exception-handler installation or operating-system
system-call interface.

PART SEVEN. PHYSICAL BINDING REQUIRED BEFORE RUNNING ON A REAL DEVICE.

A concrete machine declaration SHALL additionally identify:
  * processor/SoC vendor, part, revision, architecture feature registers,
    execution mode, supported instructions and optional extensions;
  * target byte ordering, alignment policies, memory mappings and access rights;
  * cache and memory hierarchy facts relevant to the proposed program;
  * actual OS/ABI, loader, stack and calling convention, and executable permissions;
  * source, compiler/assembler and machine-code identity;
  * observed execution evidence if actual execution is claimed.

No DRAM model number, processor part number, or bus/channel identity from
a hypothetical introductory example is asserted here. The named target is
symbolic until a target-specific manifest is attached and verified.

PART EIGHT. EXPLICIT EXCLUSIONS AND UNRESOLVED CHOICES.

This source does not define all instructions available to ARMv7-A hardware.
This source does not authorize execution in A32 (ARM) state, privileged modes,
coprocessor state, Advanced SIMD, VFP, direct device-memory control, or
an arbitrary instruction encoded in user-supplied bytes. Those capabilities
require their own enumerated grants and detailed rules.

Open questions include: whether the language's execution index is directly
manipulable as an ordinary first-class value; whether code-data conversion
can be made safe and explicit; how exceptions are typed; how concurrency and
memory ordering are represented; and how a language-level conditional
operation corresponds to T32 predicates, branches, or software dispatch.

These choices must be recorded, not silently resolved by the first compiler.
